\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2+4x|-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-4x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2+4x}{2-4x}|+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{2+4x}{2-4x}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm." 13194139533891 true] [17592186263886 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263887 13194139533891 true] [17592186263886 1089 "q-113533" 13194139533891 true] [17592186263886 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533891 true] [17592186263886 1100 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." 13194139533891 true] [17592186263886 1113 #uuid "a2b73c30-360c-5ff5-b1c4-6d6aa37e868f" 13194139533891 true] [17592186263886 1145 "First, let $u=3x+4.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+C & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|).\n$$" 13194139533891 true] [17592186263887 1000 #ed